vine 1.2.0
prefix: library
title: Library-first contract and contamination remediation
---
[root] Delineate the library contract and remove application contamination (complete)
Files: library-boundary.vine; proposals/library-contract.md; Cargo.toml; crates/**; tests/**; evidence/**; README.md; index.md; roadmap.md
Do: Establish the reusable library as the product boundary, classify every current crate and evidence surface, settle the exact public contracts, and remediate production dependency edges that make library code depend on the Caravan reference or a target application. Preserve the existing temporal and presentation invariants while treating Caravan and the desktop executable as consumers and evidence.
Accept: One library contract names public inputs, outputs, ownership, errors, time, persistence, query, branch, and presentation rules; every workspace component has one owner class; forbidden production dependency directions are explicit; generic library crates contain no Caravan or target types; the reference game and desktop sample consume only approved public surfaces; architecture guards and external-consumer evidence pass; and no target-factory or sample convenience abstraction is promoted without a named library requirement.
-> classification
-> core-contract
-> core-remediation
-> dependency-audit
-> documentation
-> evidence
-> facade-contract
-> host-contract
-> reference-contract
-> sample-remediation
> The reusable library is the product. Caravan domain/rules, Caravan orchestration, and target-local desktop execution are reference or sample consumers unless a contract task proves a reusable boundary.
> A library production dependency may not depend on caravan-*, caravan-demo, nworlds-desktop, winit, wgpu, or target metadata. Test-only dependencies may use a reference implementation only from consumer/evidence crates and may not shape production APIs.
> Generic SDK values may carry opaque game payloads, but generic library production code does not name Caravan payloads, rules, geometry, fixtures, or reference projection types.
> nworlds-host is a separate target-neutral host library boundary. It owns passive input, byte-storage, lifecycle/resource, and render-sink ports; it does not own game meaning, authoritative time, or target selection.
> The authoritative library equations remain Worldline + LogicalTime -> GameState and GameState + Tau -> owned presentation output. Host execution time, device state, and application loop policy remain outside those equations.
> The target-factory workflow remains downstream of this contract; nworlds test, nworlds run, nworlds package, target resolution, and artifact minting do not define the temporal library.
